    <ShortLegislativeSummaryEn>&lt;div&gt;A legislative summary is currently being prepared for this bill by the Parliamentary Information and Research Service of the Library of Parliament. Meanwhile, the following executive summary is available.&lt;br/&gt;On June 15 2010, the Minister of Public Safety introduced Bill C-39, the Ending Early Release for Criminals and Increasing Offender Accountability Act, in the House of Commons and it was given first reading.&lt;br/&gt;The bill amends the Corrections and Conditional Release Act. Among other things it:&lt;br/&gt;•	Clarifies that the protection of society is the paramount consideration in the corrections process for the Correctional Service of Canada and the National Parole Board and the provincial parole boards. &lt;br/&gt;•	Provides that a correctional plan is to include the level of intervention by the Service in respect of the offender's needs and the objectives for the offender's behaviour, their participation in programs and the meeting of their court-ordered obligations.&lt;br/&gt;•	Expands the range of disciplinary offences to include intimidation, false claims and throwing a bodily substance.&lt;br/&gt;•	Permits victims to make a statement at parole hearings.&lt;br/&gt;•	Permits the disclosure to a victim of the name and location of the institution to which the offender is transferred, the reason for a transfer, information about the offender's participation in programs and convictions for serious disciplinary offences and the reason for a temporary absence or a hearing waiver.&lt;br/&gt;•	Eliminates accelerated parole review.&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for the automatic suspension of the parole or statutory release of offenders who receive a new custodial sentence and require the National Parole Board to review their case within a prescribed period.&lt;br/&gt;•	Authorizes a peace officer to arrest without warrant an offender for a breach of a condition of their conditional release.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummaryEn>
    <ShortLegislativeSummaryFr>&lt;div&gt;Le Service d'information et de recherche parlementaires de la Bibliothèque du Parlement rédige actuellement le résumé législatif de ce projet de loi. Entre-temps, le Service met à votre disposition le sommaire suivant.&lt;br/&gt;Le 15 juin 2010, le ministre de la Sécurité publique a introduit le projet de loi C-39, Loi supprimant la libération anticipée des délinquants et accroissant leur responsabilité, à la Chambre des communes en première lecture.&lt;br/&gt;Le projet de loi modifie la Loi sur le système correctionnel et la mise en liberté sous condition. Il a notamment pour effet :&lt;br/&gt;•	De préciser comme critère prépondérant dans le cadre du processus correctionnel par le Service correctionnel du Canada et par la Commission Nationale des libérations conditionnelles et les commissions provinciales celui de la « protection de la société ». &lt;br/&gt;•	De prévoir que le plan correctionnel comprend le niveau d'intervention du Service à l'égard des besoins et des objectifs du délinquant en ce qui a trait à son comportement, à sa participation aux programmes et à l'exécution de ses obligations découlant d'ordonnances judiciaires.&lt;br/&gt;•	D'inclure l'intimidation, la présentation de fausses réclamations et le fait de lancer une substance corporelle comme étant des infractions disciplinaires.&lt;br/&gt;•	De permettre aux victimes d'intervenir lors des audiences sur la libération conditionnelle des délinquants.&lt;br/&gt;•	D'autoriser la communication de certaines informations relatives aux délinquants aux victimes concernées quant au nom et emplacement de l'établissement où les délinquants sont transférés, des motifs de transfère, des programmes auxquels ils participent, des infractions disciplinaires graves commises et des motifs de leurs absences temporaires ou de leur renonciation à une audience.&lt;br/&gt;•	De supprimer la procédure d'examen expéditif.&lt;br/&gt;•	De prévoir la suspension automatique de la libération conditionnelle ou de la liberté d'office des délinquants qui sont condamnés à une peine d'emprisonnement supplémentaire et l'examen de leur cas par la Commission nationale des libérations conditionnelles dans le délai réglementaire.&lt;br/&gt;•	D'autoriser un agent de la paix à arrêter sans mandat un délinquant pour une violation d'une condition de sa remise en liberté.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummaryFr>
    <ShortLegislativeSummary>&lt;div&gt;A legislative summary is currently being prepared for this bill by the Parliamentary Information and Research Service of the Library of Parliament. Meanwhile, the following executive summary is available.&lt;br/&gt;On June 15 2010, the Minister of Public Safety introduced Bill C-39, the Ending Early Release for Criminals and Increasing Offender Accountability Act, in the House of Commons and it was given first reading.&lt;br/&gt;The bill amends the Corrections and Conditional Release Act. Among other things it:&lt;br/&gt;•	Clarifies that the protection of society is the paramount consideration in the corrections process for the Correctional Service of Canada and the National Parole Board and the provincial parole boards. &lt;br/&gt;•	Provides that a correctional plan is to include the level of intervention by the Service in respect of the offender's needs and the objectives for the offender's behaviour, their participation in programs and the meeting of their court-ordered obligations.&lt;br/&gt;•	Expands the range of disciplinary offences to include intimidation, false claims and throwing a bodily substance.&lt;br/&gt;•	Permits victims to make a statement at parole hearings.&lt;br/&gt;•	Permits the disclosure to a victim of the name and location of the institution to which the offender is transferred, the reason for a transfer, information about the offender's participation in programs and convictions for serious disciplinary offences and the reason for a temporary absence or a hearing waiver.&lt;br/&gt;•	Eliminates accelerated parole review.&lt;br/&gt;•	Provides for the automatic suspension of the parole or statutory release of offenders who receive a new custodial sentence and require the National Parole Board to review their case within a prescribed period.&lt;br/&gt;•	Authorizes a peace officer to arrest without warrant an offender for a breach of a condition of their conditional release.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummary>
